<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dialogue of a Man and His Ba</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dispute between a Man and His Ba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dialogue of a Man and His Ba is an Egyptian literary text of the Middle Kingdom, preserved on a single <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. In it a man weary of life argues with his ba, his soul, about death and suicide, in a mixture of prose and poems. It is one of the earliest reflective works on the meaning of life and death.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The text was probably written during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii">Amenemhat III</a> in the Middle Kingdom. Its composition is earlier than that, since corrections and misreadings in the surviving text show it to be a copy. It belongs to the genre of sebayt, a form of Egyptian wisdom literature, and takes the form of a dialogue between a man struggling with the hardship of life and his ba. The Egyptian concept of the soul had nine parts, of which the ba, commonly translated as &quot;soul&quot;, was thought to represent a person's psyche or personality and to live on after death, able to move between the physical and spiritual worlds. Egyptian art usually shows the ba as a human-headed saddle-billed stork.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The text concerns the inner struggle of a man who is drawn to death as a release from his troubles but fears that a premature death might harm his afterlife. The surviving part contains three speeches by the man and four by the ba. At first the ba urges death as an escape from hardship, and the man objects that a premature death would deny him a proper afterlife. On reflecting on burial and funeral rites, though, they seem less permanent and more futile, and the ba then urges him to ignore his misery and embrace life.</p><p>The man replies with four litanies on social injustice, the lack of help from others, the attraction of death as release and the fortunate state of the afterlife. The roles reverse, and in its last speech the ba compromises: death should be accepted as the end of life, not as an immediate solution, so that the man and the ba can reach a proper afterlife together.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> was bought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1843 by the German Egyptologist Karl Richard Lepsius and belongs to the Ägyptisches Museum und Papyrussammlung of the Berlin State Museums. The first edition appeared in 1859, and the text has since been translated into English at least 26 times, with widely differing interpretations. The traditional reading takes it as a commentary on suicide and the funerary cult. More recent scholars read it as a psychological struggle to accept the sorrows and the goodness of life, and some see the man's turmoil as a metaphor for the unstable politics of the 12th Dynasty after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-intermediate-period">First Intermediate Period</a>.</p><p>In 2017 Papyrus Mallorca II was identified as part of the same <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, supplying a third-person introduction that calls the speaker &quot;the sick man&quot; and names a woman, Ankhet, who now seems to have been an audience for the debate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dispute Between a Man and His Ba” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Miriam Lichtheim saw it as opening a sceptical strand of thought that is also found in the <b>Dispute between a Man and His Ba</b> and in other Harper's Songs.</blockquote><a href="/songs/harpers-songs">Harper's Songs</a></li></ul></section>
